  "watch": [
    "Square colours are invisible to the trigger: this fires for any bishop-each census with a White passed pawn, including same-coloured-bishop positions where none of these plans apply. Verify opposite colours on the board; if the bishops share a colour, use the good-versus-bad entry.",
    "'Up a pawn' is a count the vocabulary lacks; the passed pawn stands in for the conversion attempt in the trigger.",
    "The drawing tendency of this family is why 'trading into it while ahead' appears as a mistake in other entries — recognizing the fortress before entering it is most of the technique.",
    "Colour convention: White is authored as the stronger/attacking side. Mirror the plans when the colours are reversed; the trigger fires only for the White-side census."
  ],
  "typicalMistakes": [
    "Trading the last rooks into an opposite-bishops ending while up a pawn — handing the defender exactly the fortress this entry describes.",
    "Blockading on the colour your bishop does not control, where the blockade can be evicted.",
    "Advancing all pawns onto one wing, where a single blockade square holds everything.",
    "As the attacker, trading pawns instead of creating the second passer."
  ],
